	'k8086cd9f' => 'Institutional premiere',
	'k82ab7e40' => 'A chronological record of our independent documentaries and commissioned works from 2021 to 2025. Each project is produced through extended presence, quiet observation, and formal rigor.',
	'k83741964' => 'Once the camera comes out, we remain unobtrusive and attentive to natural light and unprompted speech. We do not stage actions or ask people to repeat themselves. The frame belongs to the reality of the room, preserving moments exactly as they unfolded.',
	'k844ad572' => 'Instead, we commit to extended observational periods, returning across changing seasons until the camera is entirely unremarked. What emerges are patient, truthful records of working communities, crafted with rigorous technical discipline, ethical clarity, and lasting respect for the people who grant us access to their daily lives.',
	'k85948170' => 'Join the field letters',
	'k865e5ff2' => 'The field notes record what happens behind the lens during extended shoots. They recount conversations at dawn on fishing docks, technical sound logs kept in heavy coastal rain by Luca Ferreira, lighting decisions made in dimly lit hospital wards, and ethical questions that arise while editing. These letters document our craft and philosophy in real time, sharing the lessons learned from living alongside subjects across multi-month filming blocks.',
	'k86dabe7f' => 'NGO / institutional film',
	'k880c1273' => 'Shape',
	'k8911eff1' => '▸ The five steps',
	'k89302eb6' => 'Stay',
	'k89dd1546' => 'Documentary participants in Utsushi Films productions are protected by formal, written access agreements negotiated by Priya Natarajan and director Ines Calder. We retain project documentation and releases in accordance with legal requirements and archival standards. If you have questions regarding personal records or wish to update your details, contact our studio at 010 482 7716 or write to Studio 3, Ropewalk Yard, 12 Netmakers\' Lane, Port Alder.',
	'k8a2dc7f5' => 'Filmed over fourteen months aboard the fishing vessel Marit, this feature documentary observes the daily labour and seasonal rhythms of a deep-water trawler crew. Directed by Ines Calder, the production required living alongside the crew at sea to document unscripted working life without artificial lighting or staged routines. Commissioned as an independent documentary, the resulting 72-minute feature premiered at Northsea Documentary Days in 2025. The film follows deckhands and officers through winter storms and port turnarounds, capturing the quiet endurance of maritime work.',
